NNAAMMEE
     rreellaayyccttll — control the relay daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rreellaayyccttll [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rreellaayyccttll program controls the relayd(8) daemon.

     The following options are available:

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use _s_o_c_k_e_t instead of the default _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_r_e_l_a_y_d_._s_o_c_k to commu‐
             nicate with relayd(8).

     The following commands are available:

     hhoosstt ddiissaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Disable a host.  Treat it as though it were always down.

     hhoosstt eennaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Enable the host.  Start checking its health again.

     llooaadd _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Reload the configuration from the specified file.

     lloogg bbrriieeff
             Disable verbose debug logging.

     lloogg vveerrbboossee
             Enable verbose debug logging.

     mmoonniittoorr
             Continuously report any changes in the host checking engine and
             the pf(4) engine.

     ppoollll    Schedule an immediate check of all hosts.

     rreeddiirreecctt ddiissaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Disable a redirection.  If it has pf(4) redirection rules in‐
             stalled, remove them.  Mark the redirection's main table and – if
             applicable – disable the backup table as well.

     rreeddiirreecctt eennaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Enable a redirection.  Mark the redirection's main table and – if
             applicable – enable the backup table as well.

     rreellooaadd  Reload the configuration file.

     sshhooww hhoossttss
             Show detailed status of hosts and tables.  It will also print the
             last error for failed host checks; see the _D_I_A_G_N_O_S_T_I_C_S section
             below.

     sshhooww rreeddiirreeccttss
             Show detailed status of redirections including the current and
             average access statistics.  The statistics will be updated every
             minute.  Redirections using the ssttiicckkyy--aaddddrreessss option will count
             the number of sticky states, not the total number of redirected
             connections.

     sshhooww rreellaayyss
             Show detailed status of relays including the current and average
             access statistics.  The statistics will be updated every minute.

     sshhooww rroouutteerrss
             Show detailed status of routers including the configured network
             routes.

     sshhooww sseessssiioonnss
             Dump the complete list of running relay sessions.

     sshhooww ssuummmmaarryy
             Display a list of all relays, redirections, routers, tables, and
             hosts.

     ttaabbllee ddiissaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Disable a table.  Consider all hosts disabled.  If it is a main
             table of a redirection which has a non-empty backup table, swap
             the contents of the pf(4) table with those of the backup table.

     ttaabbllee eennaabbllee [_n_a_m_e | _i_d]
             Enable a table.  Start doing checks for all hosts that aren't in‐
             dividually disabled again.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/run/relayd.sock    UNIX-domain socket used for communication with
                             relayd(8).

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     If a host is down and a previous check failed, rreellaayyccttll will display the
     last error in the output of the sshhooww hhoossttss command.  This is especially
     useful for debugging server or configuration failures.  The following er‐
     rors will be reported:

     _n_o_n_e    No specific error was reported by the check engine.

     _a_b_o_r_t_e_d
             All checks were aborted by an external event, like a configura‐
             tion reload.

     _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
             The check did not finish in the configured time of an interval.
             This can happen if there are too many hosts that have to be
             checked by relayd(8) and can be avoided by increasing the global
             iinntteerrvvaall option in relayd.conf(5).

     _i_c_m_p _r_e_a_d _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_l_s _r_e_a_d _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_c_p _r_e_a_d _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
             The check failed because the remote host did not send a reply
             within the configured timeout.

     _i_c_m_p _w_r_i_t_e _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_l_s _w_r_i_t_e _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_c_p _w_r_i_t_e _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_l_s _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
     _t_c_p _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
             The check failed because relayd(8) was not ready to send the re‐
             quest within the configured timeout.

     _t_l_s _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _e_r_r_o_r
     _t_l_s _r_e_a_d _e_r_r_o_r
     _t_l_s _w_r_i_t_e _e_r_r_o_r
     _t_c_p _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _e_r_r_o_r
     _t_c_p _r_e_a_d _f_a_i_l_e_d
     _t_c_p _w_r_i_t_e _f_a_i_l_e_d
             An I/O error occurred.  This indicates that relayd(8) was running
             low on resources, file descriptors, or was too busy to run the
             request.  It can also indicate that a TLS or TCP protocol error
             occurred or that the connection was unexpectedly aborted.

     _t_l_s _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _f_a_i_l_e_d
     _t_c_p _c_o_n_n_e_c_t _f_a_i_l_e_d
             The check failed because the protocol handshake did not succeed
             in opening a stateful connection with the remote host.

     _s_c_r_i_p_t _f_a_i_l_e_d
             The external script executed by the check did not return a valid
             return code.

     _s_e_n_d_/_e_x_p_e_c_t _f_a_i_l_e_d
             The payload data returned by the remote host did not match the
             expected pattern.

     _h_t_t_p _c_o_d_e _m_a_l_f_o_r_m_e_d
     _h_t_t_p _d_i_g_e_s_t _m_a_l_f_o_r_m_e_d
             The remote host did not return a valid HTTP header or body.

     _h_t_t_p _c_o_d_e _m_i_s_m_a_t_c_h
             The remote host did not return a matching HTTP error code.  This
             may indicate a real server problem (a server error, the page was
             not found, permission was denied) or a configuration error.  For
             example, it is a very common mistake that relayd(8) was config‐
             ured to expect a HTTP 200 OK status but the host is returning a
             HTTP 302 Found redirection.  See relayd.conf(5) for more informa‐
             tion on validating the HTTP return code.

     _h_t_t_p _d_i_g_e_s_t _m_i_s_m_a_t_c_h
             The remote host did not return the expected content and the com‐
             puted digest was different to the configured value.  See
             relayd.conf(5) for more information on validating the digest.

